> tl;dr
>
> - Unified platform foundations (contentHub + VF 2.0) enabled shared components and dynamic content.
> - Faster iteration for teams; clearer paths for adoption across sites.
> - Delivered through collaboration across engineering, editorial and design with clear product leadership.

This case study distills how the Corporate Content Platform and Visual Framework worked together to create a flexible base for EMBL.org and beyond. The result: shared components that don’t fight local needs and content operations that scale.

## Problem

Multiple properties needed to share patterns and infrastructure without creating a brittle monolith or cloning code.

## Approach

- Build a “platform + design system” pairing (contentHub + VF 2.0).
- Treat components as products with docs, versions, and governance.
- Keep adoption incremental: opt‑in patterns and tokens.

## Outcomes

- Quicker delivery for new sections; more consistent UX.
- Lower maintenance cost through reuse and versioned updates.

